\subsection{E3: Threat-Driven Security}

Fourteen threat-driven attacks were enumerated and tested: replay,
audience confusion, signature forgery, payload tampering (scope
escalation, subject substitution, expiration extension), missing
signature component, delegation privilege escalation, delegation audience
mismatch, revocation bypass, expired credential, not-yet-valid credential,
algorithm downgrade (strip PQ component), and algorithm downgrade (spoof
tier tag). All 14 attacks were rejected. Each attack maps to a specific
design mechanism: nonce single-use (replay), \texttt{aud} field binding
(audience confusion), SM2 ECDSA over $Z_A \parallel M$ (forgery), signature
coverage of \texttt{scope}/\texttt{sub}/\texttt{exp} (tampering), signature
existence check (missing component), scope convergence (delegation
escalation), chain audience consistency (delegation mismatch), Merkle
accumulator revocation (bypass), \texttt{exp} freshness check (expired),
\texttt{nbf} validity check (not-yet-valid), fail-closed PQ component
requirement (downgrade strip), and algorithm-tier coverage by signature
(downgrade spoof).

\subsection{E4: Hybrid Fail-Closed}

The hybrid tier was tested under four conditions: both components valid
(accepted), SM2 component tampered (rejected), ML-DSA component tampered
(rejected), both tampered (rejected). The fail-closed property holds.

\subsection{E5: KAT Conformance}

Known-answer tests across five primitive families: SM2/SM3 (4/4 vectors),
SHA3/SHAKE (5/5), SM9 (28/28), ML-DSA-65 (9/9), SLH-DSA (8/8).
All 54 KATs passed (54/54).

\subsection{E6: Real-Baseline Comparison}

On the same machine, agent-cryptid (SM2 tier) was compared with
JWT-like tokens, Macaroons, UCAN (4-hop), and DID-VC. The comparison
covers credential size, issuance/verification latency, delegation
capability, revocation mechanism, and post-quantum readiness.

\begin{table*}[t]
  \caption{Real-baseline comparison on the same host (loaded).}
  \label{tab:realbaseline}
  \centering\small\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
  \begin{tabular}{lccccc}
    \toprule
    \textbf{Scheme} & \textbf{Size (B)} & \textbf{Issue (ms)} & \textbf{Verify (ms)} & \textbf{Delegation} & \textbf{PQ} \\
    \midrule
    JWT-like (single sig) & 220 & 74.8 & 85.1 & None & No \\
    Macaroon (HMAC chain) & 163 & 4.1 & 4.1 & Chain (attenuation) & No \\
    UCAN (chained sig, 4-hop) & 3438 & 326.9 & 342.0 & Chain (sig) & No \\
    DID-VC (sig + status-list) & 276 & 77.5 & 79.4 & Shallow & No \\
    \midrule
    agent-cryptid (SM2) & 449 & 96.8 & 102.8 & Chain + scope convergence & Yes \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Agent-cryptid is the only scheme that simultaneously provides native
delegation chains with cryptographic scope convergence, offline
revocation via Merkle accumulators, and post-quantum readiness through
its three-tier agility architecture.

\paragraph{A caveat on latency comparison: primitives differ.}
A reviewer will immediately note the 25$\times$ verification gap versus
Macaroons. This comparison conflates three distinct primitives and
should be read with that in mind.

\emph{(i)~Asymmetric single-signature.} JWT-like, DID-VC, and
agent-cryptid (SM2) all perform one asymmetric signature verification
per credential. Their latencies cluster tightly at 79--103~ms, i.e.,
agent-cryptid pays a 1.2--1.3$\times$ premium over the fastest
asymmetric baseline for scope convergence, audience binding, tier
binding, and offline revocation---a small price for the additional
cryptographic commitments.

\emph{(ii)~Symmetric HMAC chain.} Macaroons achieve 4.1~ms because
verification is HMAC-based: symmetric-key operations dominate, and the
verifier holds the root key. This makes Macaroons fast \emph{but
disqualifies them from a security model that requires asymmetric
verification}---there is no third-party verifiability and no
non-repudiation, since any key holder can issue a Macaroon. A direct
latency comparison with an asymmetric-verifiable credential is
therefore comparing different threat models, not different
implementations of the same primitive.

\emph{(iii)~Multi-hop signature chain, matched at equal depth.}
The 342~ms UCAN figure in Table~\ref{tab:realbaseline} is a 4-hop
chain, while agent-cryptid's 102.8~ms is a single-hop credential---an
apples-to-oranges pairing. To correct this, we measured agent-cryptid
on a matched 4-hop delegation chain (root + three delegations, each
signed by a distinct agent identity): median verification
\textbf{190~ms} with p99 at 452~ms, versus UCAN's 631~ms with p99 at
831~ms. Agent-cryptid is \textbf{3.3$\times$ faster per hop} at equal
chain depth (47.5~ms/hop vs.\ 157.9~ms/hop). The gap comes from
protocol structure rather than signature speed (both use SM2 as the
asymmetric primitive): agent-cryptid's scope convergence and audience
binding are checked as set-containment operations inside a single
verify pass, whereas UCAN re-serialises each hop body and issues a
fresh asymmetric verification with no shared context.

\begin{table}[h!]
\caption{Verification cost decomposed by underlying cryptographic primitive.
``4h'' denotes a delegation chain at equal depth to the UCAN baseline.
``Asym.'' marks whether third-party asymmetric verification is possible
(Macaroon requires the verifier to hold the root HMAC key and therefore
has no asymmetric-verifiable mode).}
\label{tab:primitive}
\centering\small\setlength{\tabcolsep}{5pt}
\begin{tabular}{lrc}
  \toprule
  \textbf{Scheme} & \textbf{Verify (ms)} & \textbf{Asym.} \\
  \midrule
  Macaroon (HMAC chain)       & 4.1    & No  \\
  JWT-like (single sig.)      & 85.1   & Yes \\
  DID-VC (single sig.)        & 79.4   & Yes \\
  \textbf{agent-cryptid} (1h) & \textbf{102.8} & Yes \\
  \textbf{agent-cryptid} (4h) & \textbf{190.0} & Yes \\
  UCAN (4h chain)             & 631.0  & Yes \\
  \bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Within each primitive class, agent-cryptid's cost is within a
respectable factor of the state of the art: 1.2--1.3$\times$ on
asymmetric single-signature, and 3.3$\times$ \emph{faster} than a
comparable 4-hop signature chain. The apparent 25$\times$ gap in the
headline comparison is an artefact of pairing an asymmetric-verifiable
credential against a symmetric-only scheme with a strictly weaker
security model.

We report an honest caveat on issuance cost: agent-cryptid's
Table~\ref{tab:realbaseline} issuance figure (96.8~ms) reflects a
single-hop credential, whereas the 4-hop chain's issuance cost in our
reference run includes per-hop database registration and is therefore
\emph{not} directly comparable to UCAN's 327~ms. Production
deployments with a warm identity registry eliminate this overhead.

